  "description": "health.v1 §5, \"is this service's clock ahead?\" (§2.5): what one subscriber heard of one service during its reading, in arrival order -> the answer. A case gives presence, descriptor and face as judgements.json writes them. heard: the deliveries, each \"heartbeat\" (a heartbeat covering health.v1 whose status's section the reader read in step, freshness.v2 §2.5's confirmed, its bit set or clear, and whose time is known), \"heartbeat_held\" (the same, its time unknown, all ones, as while the guard holds: freshness.v2 §2.10), \"status_put\" (a put of state/status, which the guard does not hold), \"status_deleted\" (a delete of state/status), \"clock_ahead\" (a stream/faults sample whose code is clock_ahead, whatever its level and its stamp) or \"fault\" (a stream/faults sample with any other code). expect.answer is yes, no, unobservable or not_asked: yes on a clock_ahead fault with no heartbeat whose time is known heard after it; no when the last heartbeat heard has its time known and came after the last clock_ahead fault, or with none heard; unobservable otherwise, since the faults' silence is their normal state and a time unknown proves nothing alone; not_asked when the service is absent or its descriptor does not list health.v1, as the first question's. A put or a delete of the status says nothing of the clock. A fault heard answers whatever else presence says. expect.reason: clock_ahead, confirmed, time_unknown, nothing_heard, absent or not_listed."
